16   Q:     Whose testimony do you discuss in your testimony?

17   A:     I am addressing aspects of the testimony of El Paso Electric Company (“EPE”) witnesses

18          Enedina Soto, Manuel Carrasco, Rene Gonzalez, Adrian Hernandez, George Novela,

19   III.   LOAD ANALYSIS AND ALLOCATOR DEVELOPMENT

20   Q:     Which EPE witness addresses load analysis and allocator development?

21   A:     EPE witness Enedina Soto addresses these topics, including EPE’s load research

22          methods, the development of jurisdiction and rate class allocation factors, and forecasts

23          of energy and demand.
                Douglas B. Jester – Direct Testimony – Page 8 of 72 – Case No. 25-00082-UT


 1   Q:      What is your overall assessment of EPE’s load analysis and allocator development?

 2   A:      In most respects, the load analysis and allocator development was done reasonably, using

 3           standard methods. There are three aspects of this work about which I have reservations:

 4           use of sample versus population load data, adjustment of distribution system allocators

 5           for distributed generation, and allocator adjustments related to dedicated resources.

 6   Q:      What is your reservation regarding use of sample versus population load data?

 7   A:      As testified by witness Soto, EPE relied on a sample of customers rather than full

 8           population data for the load analysis of rate schedules NM-01, NM-03, NM-04, NM-05,

 9           NM-07, NM-08, and NM-25 in the present case.1 They did so in part because the AMS

10           rollout that would have provided data for a population-based approach to load analysis

11           was not complete at the start of the base period used in the analysis.2


12           Witness Soto also presented comparisons of sample and population data for each of the

13           sampled rate schedules except NM-25, concluding “that the population-based approach is

14           consistent with the traditional sample-based results.”3 I examined the data presented is

15           Exhibit ES-1 for the possible effects of this conclusion. Figure 1 below shows the

16           differences between the key statistics of per customer values for annual energy,

17           maximum class demand, 4CP coincident demand, 12CP coincident demand, and non-

18           coincident peak demand.




