 7              having higher values than the population, while the monthly population energy values

 8              exceed the sample values in the last months of the base period for N-03 and the

 9              differences between the monthly energy value from the population and the sample are

10              consistent across the base period for rate classes N-04, N-05, and N-08. The population

11              monthly energy values for rate class N-07, on the other hand, exceed the sample monthly

12              energy values only in the early months of the base period.4 Overall, this is suggestive but

13              not determinative that there are real differences between the population and sample that

14              may manifest as higher cost allocation to residential customers when the cost of service

15              study is based on the load research sample.

16   Q:         How should the Commission respond to your reservation regarding use of sample

17              versus population data in EPE’s load research?

18   A:         I recommend that the Commission accept EPE’s load research results based on customer

19              samples for purposes of this case, but with an awareness that it may cause an

20              overallocation of costs to residential customers and a corresponding under-allocation of

21              costs to most other customer classes. I also recommend that the Commission direct EPE




     4
         See the graphs in Exhibit ES-1.
            Douglas B. Jester – Direct Testimony – Page 11 of 72 – Case No. 25-00082-UT


 1        to use population-based load research in future cases unless EPE demonstrates a

 2        compelling argument for continued use of sample-based load research.

 3   Q:   Why do you recommend that approach?

 4   A:   It is a practical impossibility to rebuild EPE’s entire case using population rather than

 5        sample load research within the confines of a rate case, and it remains possible that there

 6        are biases in the population data that result from the incomplete rollout of the AMS data

 7        at the start of the base period.


 8        But, population data has several merits that were apparently not considered by EPE.

 9        Population data will be the same metering data on which billing is based, so there will be

10        consistency between the bases for cost allocation and billing. Using population data for

11        load research opens the way for the Commission to order special studies, such as

12        comparing cost of service for low-income households to their bills or evaluating results

13        of energy efficiency measures, and for Staff and intervenors to obtain samples or

14        complete data for supplemental load analyses during cases that would not be practical, or

15        would not provide sufficient sample sizes, based on load research samples and might then

16        not be comparable to the load research samples,.

17   Q:   What is your reservation regarding the adjustment of distribution system allocators

18        for distributed generation?

19   A:   Witness Soto explains in this case that historically EPE made an adjustment to the per

20        book billing determinants to account for the total energy delivered to DG customers

21        rather than using net energy, but in this case has used net energy for production and
                  Douglas B. Jester – Direct Testimony – Page 12 of 72 – Case No. 25-00082-UT


 1              transmission allocation factors.5 However, she also explains that for purposes of

 2              distribution allocation factors EPE continues to add into the allocator statistics the energy

 3              received by EPE from its DG customers. Witness Soto asserts that the “adjustment is

 4              appropriate because distribution costs are a function of all energy delivered to each

 5              customer’s service point regardless of the type of customer.”6 I consider the addition of

 6              energy outflow from the customer to EPE back into the allocator statistics for distribution

 7              to be incorrect.

 8   Q:         Why do you consider adding energy outflow from the customer to EPE back into

 9              the distribution allocator statistics to be incorrect?

10   A:         Witness Soto is correct that EPE delivers to the customer that portion of their

11              consumption that is offset in their bill by their outflow. However, it is not accurate to

12              think that delivery of that energy uses the distribution system in a way that is not offset

13              by the customer’s outflow.

14   Q:         What is the effect of distributed generation on power flows in the distribution

15              system?

16   A:         Distributed generation, more specifically behind-the-meter generation, produces power

17              and injects it into circuits within the customer premises. Within those circuits, power from

18              distributed generation flows to the end uses of power, or loads, on the customer premises.

19              If the amount of power supplied from the distributed generation is less than the amount

20              required by the customer’s loads, additional power will be drawn from the utility to meet

21              the total load requirement; this may be referred to as inflow. If the amount of power




     5
         Direct testimony of Enedina Soto 13:18 – 14:14
     6
         Direct testimony of Enedina Soto 15:6-10
            Douglas B. Jester – Direct Testimony – Page 13 of 72 – Case No. 25-00082-UT


 1        supplied from the distributed generation is greater than the amount required by the

 2        customer’s loads, the excess distributed generation will flow from the customer’s premises

 3        to the utility; I am referring to this as outflow. At any instant, power will either flow from

 4        the utility to the customer or from the customer to the utility but not both directions.


 5        Outflow to the utility from a customer that has distributed generation flows through the

 6        meter and through the service line that connects the customer to the utility’s distribution

 7        system. At the point where the customer’s service line connects to the distribution system,

 8        outflow will either join normal down-current flows to serve down-current customers while

 9        reducing flows from up-current to the point of customer interconnection or will be larger

10        than any down-current loads and reverse the normal direction of flow. At some point in the

11        power flow looking higher up in the distribution system, the outflow from the customer

12        having distributed generation will be less than the flow it joins and will simply reduce the

13        flow from up-current to that point of interconnection.


14        Witness Soto recognizes that at the level of generation and transmission, the outflow

15        from distributed generation simply reduces power flow toward customers but fails to

16        recognize that is also true of much of the distribution system.

17   Q:   How does outflow affect the distribution system?

18   A:   Outflow from a DG customer does flow over the service line, but further up in the

19        distribution system it depends on the aggregate effect of outflow from all customers on

20        that portion of the distribution system. At modest levels of DG adoption, the outflow will

21        almost certainly go to another customer on the same secondary circuit, reducing flow

22        through the line transformer and over part of the secondary circuit, and also reducing
            Douglas B. Jester – Direct Testimony – Page 14 of 72 – Case No. 25-00082-UT


 1        flow on the primary circuit to the line transformer and through the substation into the

 2        primary circuit. Outflow therefore reduces the use of the distribution system in all but

 3        very local segments.

 4   Q:   How should DG outflow be considered in EPE’s cost of service study?

 5   A:   Service lines are allocated as customer costs, so that cost allocation is not affected by the

 6        treatment of outflow. At modest adoption levels, DG outflow reduces power flows on a

 7        portion of the secondary system, and through the entire distribution system from

 8        transmission through the local transformer. I therefore recommend that the DG

 9        adjustment posited by EPE not be used and that net energy to customers in each class be

10        used to allocate distribution system costs.


11        If, in future, EPE shows that DG adoption levels are reaching levels that reverse flows in

12        layers of the distribution system hierarchy above the secondary lines, the DG adjustment

13        can be applied in the layers that experience the reverse flow by considering the absolute

14        value of net energy as the basis for the allocator for that layer. In other words, when

15        outflows are high enough to reverse flow through the line transformer, it would be

16        appropriate to allocate costs for the secondary system and secondary line transformers

17        based on the absolute value of flows at that time, but it would still not be appropriate to

18        apply the DG adjustment for cost allocation of substations or primary circuits until

19        reverse flows began to appear there.

20   Q:   How can this be dealt with in the present case?

21   A:   In the present case, the effects of EPE’s choice on this are small and difficult to revise

22        within the constraints of this case, so I recommend accepting EPE’s treatment in this case

23        while ordering that future cases be handled as I described above.
                  Douglas B. Jester – Direct Testimony – Page 15 of 72 – Case No. 25-00082-UT


 1   Q:         What is your reservation regarding allocator adjustments related to dedicated

 2              resources?

 3   A:         Witness Soto explains that EPE adjusted its jurisdictional allocators by constructing the

 4              usual allocator statistics and then removing from those energy and demand statistics the

 5              contributions of the dedicated resources. The jurisdictional allocation was then based on

 6              the net of load and dedicated resources for each jurisdiction.7 That may currently be a

 7              reasonable approach but it will need to be adjusted within a few years.


 8              A significant portion of the jurisdictionally dedicated resources, or of customer-dedicated

 9              resources that should also count as jurisdictionally dedicated resources for the customer’s

10              jurisdiction, will be renewable generation and storage. This will result in the timing of

11              system critical peaks net of dedicated resources being different than total load peaks. It

12              will be the loads net of dedicated resources that will determine the needs for system

13              resources and hence should be the basis for allocation of costs for non-jurisdictional

14              resources. The difference is conceptually subtle, but because dedicated resources change

15              the timing of the net peaks to be served by non-dedicated resources, the jurisdictional

16              shares of peak-based allocators may also change.


17              Additionally, the only value of storage attributed in the current approach to jurisdictional

18              allocation is reduction of peak demands, but storage systems are typically operating daily

