11    V.     COST OF SERVICE ALLOCATION – HOURLY COST PARADIGM

12   Q:      What is an hourly cost method for cost allocation?

13   A:      The fundamental idea of the hourly cost method is to allocate utility costs for each

14           resource in each function to the hours of the year, proportionate to usage in each hour,

15           then add up the costs across resources in each hour and allocate those to customer classes

16           in proportion to each class’s usage in that hour. Similar ideas have been used in the past;

17           for example the NARUC manual13 includes a way called the “probability of dispatch”

18           method to allocate production costs that is conceptually similar. However, data for such

19           methods was sparse so practice centered on ways to combine energy and various critical

20           peak metrics. Modern advanced customer metering and grid automation, using

21           technologies such as SCADA, now provide substantial data at hourly or finer granularity



     13
       National Association of Regulatory Utility Commissioners. Electric Utility Cost Allocation Manual. Washington,
     D.C.: NARUC, 1992.
               Douglas B. Jester – Direct Testimony – Page 30 of 72 – Case No. 25-00082-UT


 1           that enables new methods of cost allocation that are both strongly empirical and better

 2           grounded in cost causation. A relatively recent publication presents strong arguments that

 3           an hourly cost method is preferred over historical practice.14


 4           In this section of my testimony, I will describe the application of this concept to each

 5           function. The Staff recommendation is that this approach be examined and considered by

 6           Commission Staff, EPE, and other stakeholders in a Staff-led process following this

 7           proceeding to establish a recommended new approach to cost allocation for EPE to

 8           present in a future case.

 9               a. Production Cost Allocation

10   Q:      How would an hourly cost method be applied to the utility’s production function?

11   A:      All revenue requirements for the production function would be further broken out by

12           resource for generation, storage, and net interchange purchases. For each such resource,

13           costs are then allocated by the hour in proportion to energy supplied in that hour. All

14           production costs in each hour are then added up and divided by energy demanded in that

15           hour (which is also the average demand in the hour) to get an hourly cost per unit energy.

16           That hourly cost can then be allocated to customer classes by multiplying the hourly unit

17           cost by class consumption at generation during that hour. Adding the cost responsibility

18           of a class for each hour of the year yields the class annual cost responsibility for

19           determination of rates.




