      Lazar, Jim, Paul Chernick, William Marcus, and Mark LeBel, ed. Electric Cost Allocation for a New Era: A
     Manual. Montpelier, VT: Regulatory Assistance Project, 2020 provided as Exhibit DBJ-2.
            Douglas B. Jester – Direct Testimony – Page 31 of 72 – Case No. 25-00082-UT


 1   Q:   How does this method work for renewable generation like wind and solar?

 2   A:   The full revenue requirement of a renewable project will be allocated to each hour of the

 3        year in proportion to production in that hour. For solar, there will be no revenue

 4        requirement allocation at night when it does not produce, and revenue requirements

 5        allocated to daylight hours will be proportional to solar irradiance as seen by the solar

 6        system. If there is curtailment, revenue requirements are assigned based on delivered

 7        power, not including the curtailed production.

 8   Q:   How does this method work for traditional “base load” plants?

 9   A:   The full revenue requirement is allocated to annual production, which will generally look

10        like revenue requirements are allocated in all hours of the year except when the plants are

11        out of operation due to maintenance or forced outages. Although these plants typically

12        have higher annual revenue requirements per unit capacity than load-following and

13        peaker plants, the revenue requirements are spread across most hours of the year and will

14        therefore have a comparatively low revenue requirement per unit of production. Hours in

15        which power supply is dominated by renewables and “base load” plants will have

16        relatively low revenue requirements per unit energy.

17   Q:   How does this method work for load-following and peaker plants?

18   A:   The full revenue requirement is allocated to annual production and to MW-hours of

19        standby availability, perhaps with an adjustment for any difference in operating costs

20        between production and standby. This results in relatively high unit revenue requirements

21        for plants that are more rarely used, adding significant revenue requirements to those

22        hours when rarely used units are called upon. Presently, those tend to be peak demand

23        hours but as the renewables share of generation increases, the hours when these plants are
            Douglas B. Jester – Direct Testimony – Page 32 of 72 – Case No. 25-00082-UT


 1        called will increasingly be “net peak” hours when the difference between demand and

 2        renewables is high.

 3   Q:   How does this method work for storage resources?

 4   A:   Storage charging becomes a part of the demand in each hour when charging occurs, and

 5        total cost of power supply during those hours will partly be allocated to the charging load.

 6        The revenue requirements allocated to the charging load become the “fuel cost” of the

 7        storage systems. When storage is quiescent, no revenue requirements are allocated to the

 8        storage as either a cost of charging or a revenue requirement for load. When storage

 9        discharges, the costs of charging and the cost of owning and operating the storage are

10        revenue requirements allocated to the discharge hours in proportion to the amount of

11        discharge. This has the effect of moving the charging costs for storage to the hours when

12        storage discharge contributes to customer supply. All storage system costs are allocated

13        to the hours of discharge in analogy to what happens with load-following and peaker

14        plants.

15   Q:   How does this method work for market operations?

16   A:   Some modification is needed since market prices are based on marginal costs rather than

17        fully allocated costs. Net wholesale market sales will be recognized as meeting some of

18        the revenue requirements during hours when such sales to the market occur, with the

19        market revenue recorded as a negative revenue requirement in totaling up the revenue

20        requirement for each hour. Similarly, the cost of net market purchases are allocated to

21        customer load or storage charging in the hours when such net market purchases occur and

22        included as revenue requirements when adding up hourly revenue requirements for

23        allocation to customer classes.
