 4        hourly load of the dedicatee when revenue requirements are being allocated to load. This

 5        works whether it is for jurisdictional allocation, or for treatment of bespoke resources like

 6        those currently provided by EPE to certain customers or provided to a future data center

 7        customer.

 8   Q:   What are the principal reasons for using an hourly cost method to allocated

 9        production costs?

10   A:   The principal reason generally is that it allocates the cost of each resource in accordance

11        with how that resource fits into the overall resource portfolio.


12        Dedicated resources are allocated to the dedicatee and the dedicatee interacts with the

13        shared resource pool on the basis of their net demand on the shared resource pool.


14        Capacity costs are allocated when they are used and not based just on demand peaks nor

15        to generic energy. In integrated resource planning, meeting capacity requirements is a

16        constraint that must be satisfied, but the use of anything other than a peaking resource

17        (which has the lowest cost per unit capacity) is justified by an ability to produce energy at

18        non-peak times more cheaply than a peaking resource. It allocates the high cost of “base

19        load” plants across the many hours when they are used rather than allocating those costs

20        based on a few critical peaks, when it was the ability to provide low-cost energy

21        throughout the year that would have justified the original investment in base-load

22        capacity. Similarly, the incremental capacity cost of load-following resources over

23        peaking resources is allocated to the shoulder-period energy that load-following resources
       Douglas B. Jester – Direct Testimony – Page 34 of 72 – Case No. 25-00082-UT


 1   provide, and which would have justified selection of load-following resources over

 2   peaking resources. Peaking resource costs are allocated to the peak hours when those

 3   occur and to the unexpected demand imbalances when the agility of peaking resources

 4   causes them to be called upon.


 5   Over time, this method will reflect the evolving use of peaking resources to serve high

 6   load net of renewables, allocating peaking resources to the gaps left by renewables; if

 7   passed through in rate design, this will encourage alignment between consumption and

 8   renewables supply.


 9   This method also better integrates with wholesale energy markets in that net interchange

10   revenue is accounted for in cost allocation based on when it is used.


11   Finally, if cost allocation to customer classes were reflected in rate designs under

12   traditional cost allocation, very high rates would be applied to a few peak hours and rates

13   in all other hours would be low. High rates would then suppress demand in those peaks

14   and other hours would become peaks. It would be nearly impossible to reflect traditional

15   cost allocations in rates because doing so would change the timing of peaks. On the other

16   hand, the current practice of designing rates in a way that is inconsistent with cost

17   allocation misleads customers as to what electricity usage is costly and gulls them into

18   unnecessarily costly behavior or investments in energy-using equipment. In contrast,

19   time-based rate design based on an hourly cost method of cost allocation provides

20   directionally correct price signals that are moderate enough to gently push customers

21   toward more economically efficient uses of electricity without creating high usage

22   volatility.
