 1           b. Transmission Cost Allocation

 2   Q:   How would an hourly cost method be applied to a utility’s transmission function?

 3   A:   To apply the hourly cost method to transmission, it is helpful to think of transmission

 4        capacity as layered, with the bottom layers being the portion of capacity that is always

 5        used and the upper layers as that portion of capacity that is only occasionally used. The

 6        incremental costs of each layer of capacity are then allocated to the hours when it is used,

 7        with the bottom layer allocated throughout the year and the capacity for peak demand

 8        allocated to the few hours of the year it is used. This approach to cost allocation creates

 9        an empirically based balance between cost allocation based on energy, established in

10        those many hours when demand is low, and cost allocation based on peak demand,

11        established in those few hours when demand is high.

12   Q:   Can you illustrate that calculation?

13   A:   Yes. I obtained EPE’s total system demand by hour for calendar year 2025 from data

14        provided as an attachment by EPE in response to City of Las Cruces discovery request 1-

15        13. In those data, the lowest hourly average total system demand was 580 MW on

16        4/20/2025 in the hour ending 10, the second lowest was 583 MW on 4/20/2025 in the

17        hour ending 11. At the other extreme, the highest hourly average total system demand

18        was 2,325 MW on 8/6/2025 in the hour ending 17 and the second highest was 2,304 on

19        8/16//2025 in the hour ending 17.


20        Using these data, the hourly cost method would spread across 8760 hours of the year the

21        cost of providing 580 MW of capacity. It would allocate across the 8759 hours of the

22        year, excluding the hour ending 10 on 4/20/2025, the costs of the 3 MW capacity that was

23        used in the hour ending 11 on 4/20/2025 and all other hours but not used in the hour
            Douglas B. Jester – Direct Testimony – Page 36 of 72 – Case No. 25-00082-UT


 1        ending 10 on 4/20/2025. The total costs allocated to the hour ending 10 on 4/20/2025

 2        would be only the cost of 580 MW divided by 8760 while the total costs allocated to the

 3        hour ending 11 on 4/20/2025 would be the cost of 580 MW divided by 8760 plus the cost

 4        of 3 MW divided by 8759.


 5        The hourly cost method would allocate the costs of the 21 MW of transmission capacity

 6        used in the hour ending 17 on 8/6/2025 but not in any other hour of the year to that hour

 7        and add it to that hour’s pro-rata share of the incremental costs of capacity in every other

 8        hour of the year. All other hours would be similarly calculated.

 9   Q:   How should the costs of each layer of capacity be determined?

10   A:   There are two approaches available within the logic of the hourly cost method. The

11        current practice of allocating all transmission revenue requirements proportional to peak

12        demand implicitly assumes that every unit of capacity has the same unit cost. The

13        Commission could take the view that all transmission costs are uniform costs of

14        transmission capacity and allocate all transmission costs using the hourly cost method.


15        As I testified above, substantial costs of transmission are attributable to the geographic

16        coverage of EPE’s service territory to deliver power from generation to load and not

17        caused by the level of capacity required. Those costs could reasonably be entirely

18        assigned to the first 580 MW transmission capacity that is used all 8760 hours of the

19        year. The remaining capacity-related costs could be assigned in proportion to units of

20        capacity used in each hour. Thus 580 MW/2325 MW of capacity-related costs would be

21        assigned as the incremental capacity cost to be spread across all 8760 hours of the year,

22        while 3MW/2325MW of capacity costs would be spread across the 8759 hours of the
            Douglas B. Jester – Direct Testimony – Page 37 of 72 – Case No. 25-00082-UT


 1        year excluding 4/20/2025 hour ending 10. Similar calculations would apply to each other

 2        hour of the year.


 3        The first of these methods emphasizes allocating costs to peak demand while the second

 4        somewhat shifts cost responsibility more toward annual energy. More explicitly, the first

 5        method allocates costs more heavily to weather-sensitive classes like residential and

 6        small commercial customers while the second method allocates costs more heavily on

 7        constant load customers.


 8        Allocating the costs of network geography across all 8760 hours of the year, the second

 9        method, better reflects cost causation since those costs are necessary in all hours while

10        costs directly related to demand can be allocated to layers of demand as described above.

11           c. Distribution Cost Allocation

12   Q:   How would an hourly cost method be applied to a utility’s distribution function?

13   A:   The method is similar to that described for transmission, applied using only the

14        distribution load. This approach to cost allocation creates an empirically based balance

15        between cost allocation based on energy, established in those many hours when demand

16        is low, and cost allocation based on peak demand, established in those few hours when

17        demand is high.

18        As with transmission, the Commission must ultimately decide as a matter of policy

19        whether to include all distribution system costs as though they are uniform per unit of

20        capacity for all layers of capacity or whether the costs of geographic reach should be

21        treated as costs of the first layer of distribution capacity and only costs of transformers

22        and other demand-related components allocated using the hourly cost method.
            Douglas B. Jester – Direct Testimony – Page 38 of 72 – Case No. 25-00082-UT


 1        Allocating the costs of network geography across all 8760 hours of the year and

 2        allocating the incremental costs of demand to the hours when it is used, the second

 3        method, better reflects cost causation since those costs are necessary in all hours.


 4        One difference between the transmission and distribution systems is that the transmission

 5        system is a single network, typically with multiple paths to each node, while the

 6        distribution system is really a series of distribution networks each connected to the

 7        transmission network. It would therefore be plausible to allocate the costs of each

 8        distribution network separately. This could inform having different rates in different

 9        places, which reflect differences in customer clustering, customer and load density, and

10        similar geographical patterns that affect distribution network costs. However, it would

11        not be particularly informative for the allocation of costs to most customer classes.

12        Residential and general service customers are generally well mixed for the reason that

13        employers, residential customers, local government infrastructure and offices, outdoor

14        lighting, and retail businesses are generally well mixed in settlements. Irrigation and

15        agricultural businesses that are not well clustered might bear greater costs if different

16        geographic distribution systems were broken out for cost allocation. Rural residential and

17        small business customers might also have higher rates than customers of the same class

18        located in settlements.

19           d. Customer Cost Allocation

20   Q:   How would an hourly cost method be applied to a utility’s customer costs?

21   A:   In general, it would not, so long as the Commission continues to use the basic cost

22        method to determine customer-related costs. That is our recommendation.
               Douglas B. Jester – Direct Testimony – Page 39 of 72 – Case No. 25-00082-UT


 1           Looked at broadly, the transmission and distribution systems approximate Steiner

 2           networks.15 Steiner networks result from minimizing total network length subject to

 3           connecting every end point (in this case customers and generation) into the network.

 4           Whether formally analyzed as a Steiner network or just done by conventional practice,

 5           the basic architecture of the power system reflects the general idea that sharing power

 6           lines amongst customers in an area reduces line miles and thereby reduces costs. In a

 7           Steiner network design, most of the line miles are to reach clusters of customers (cities,

 8           villages, etc.) and only small additions to the network are needed to reach each customer.

 9           These small additions are well-proxied by the service lines that connect customers to

10           distribution lines. Additional costs of metering, billing, and customer service are also

11           driven by the customer count. These are the costs that are driven by customer count and

12           included in the basic customer charge. These costs are only trivially driven by energy or

13           demand, so application of the hourly cost method is not necessary.

