 3   A:         EPE proposes to initiate a Peak Time Rebate (“PTR”) pilot program.23 In this program,

 4              EPE could announce up to 20 events per summer and would provide a bill credit to

 5              residential customers that reduce their energy usage during the 3pm-7pm peak hours

 6              during each event. Customer compensation would be based on the reduction in the

 7              customer’s usage during the PTR event compared to typical consumption on similar non-

 8              PTR event days.24 The compensation rate would be EPE’s avoided cost of energy

 9              calculated under Rate No. 16. EPE requests an accounting order to defer costs of the

10              pilot, including customer compensation.


11              Staff support EPE undertaking this pilot as they continue to work toward using demand-

12              side resources to meet resource adequacy. However, Staff cautions that evaluation of this

13              pilot needs to account for variation in individual demand. Natural variation in energy use,

14              such as the number of people at home, activities within the home, etc., will cause

15              customers to use varying amounts from day-to-day even if weather conditions are similar.

16              This can result in compensating customers for using less when they did not do so

17              intentionally. That is an acceptable outcome, but the overall program benefits must be

18              commensurate with its costs. EPE will need to demonstrate that the reduced demand is

19              not illusory in order to justify continuation of this program after the pilot. This

20              information should be filed by EPE as a compliance item at the conclusion of the pilot in

21              this or another appropriate docket as determined by the Commission.



